    "abstract": "Abstract We present TerraClimate, a dataset of high-spatial resolution (1/24\u00b0, ~4-km) monthly climate and climatic water balance for global terrestrial surfaces from 1958\u20132015. TerraClimate uses climatically aided interpolation, combining high-spatial resolution climatological normals from the WorldClim dataset, with coarser resolution time varying (i.e., monthly) data from other sources to produce a monthly dataset of precipitation, maximum and minimum temperature, wind speed, vapor pressure, and solar radiation. TerraClimate additionally produces monthly surface water balance datasets using a water balance model that incorporates reference evapotranspiration, precipitation, temperature, and interpolated plant extractable soil water capacity. These data provide important inputs for ecological and hydrological studies at global scales that require high spatial resolution and time varying climate and climatic water balance data. We validated spatiotemporal aspects of TerraClimate using annual temperature, precipitation, and calculated reference evapotranspiration from station data, as well as annual runoff from streamflow gauges. TerraClimate datasets showed noted improvement in overall mean absolute error and increased spatial realism relative to coarser resolution gridded datasets.",
    "field": "Climate data modeling",
    "problem": "Existing global climate datasets lack the high spatial resolution and long-term monthly variability needed for ecological and hydrological studies, as they are typically either static, high-resolution climatologies or time-varying but at a coarse spatial resolution, creating a critical data gap for fine-scale analysis.",
    "method": "This paper presents a method that creates a high-resolution global climate dataset by using climatically aided interpolation to combine high-resolution climatologies with coarser, time-varying climate data, and subsequently applies a water balance model to produce additional climatic variables.",
    "task": "The task is to produce a high-resolution global dataset of monthly climate and climatic water balance from 1958\u20132015 by combining high-resolution climatologies with coarser, time-varying climate data from other sources.",
